                    Referred to: Nelles v. Ontario, [1989] 2 S.C.R. 170; Proulx v. Quebec (Attorney General), 2001 SCC 66, [2001] 3 S.C.R. 9; Krieger v. Law Society of Alberta, <b>2002 SCC 65</b>, [2002] 3 S.C.R. 372; R. v. R. (D.) (1995), 98 C.C.C. (3d) 353, rev’d [1996] 2 S.C.R. 291; Heath v. Heape (1856), 1 H. &amp; N. 478, 156 E.R. 1289; Hicks v.&nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;When taken by a Crown prosecutor, this decision is one of the “core elements” of prosecutorial discretion, thus lying “beyond the legitimate reach of the court” under the constitutionally entrenched principle of independence: Krieger v. Law Society of Alberta, <b>2002 SCC 65</b>, [2002] 3 S.C.R. 372, at paras. 32 and 46. The 
                </p>
                <div class="spacer">
                        <span class="decisia-icon"></span>
                        <span class="decisia-icon"></span>
                </div>
        </div> 
        <div class="documents">
