<div class="metadata">
    <div class="info" >
        <div class="subinfo">
            <h3>
                <span class="title"><a target="_parent" href="/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/2340/index.do?q=%222002+SCC+65%22">R. v. Beaudry</a></span>
                        - <span class="citation">2007 SCC 5</span>
                    - <span class="report-citation">[2007] 1 SCR 190</span>
                - <span class="publicationDate">2007-01-31</span>
            </h3>
            <div class="subMetadata">
                <span>Supreme Court Judgments</span>
            </div> 
            <div class="subject">
                    <span>
                            Criminal law<br/>
                    </span>
            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                    C.C.C. (3d) 225; R. v. Campbell, [1999] 1 S.C.R. 565; R. v. Regan, [2002] 1 S.C.R. 297, 2002 SCC 12; Krieger v. Law Society of Alberta, [2002] 3 S.C.R. 372, <b>2002 SCC 65</b>; R. v. May (1984), 13 C.C.C. (3d) 257; R. v. Hearn (1989), 48 C.C.C. (3d) 376, aff’d [1989] 2 S.C.R. 1180; R. v. Charbonneau (1992), 13 C.R. (4th) 191; R. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;In discharging their respective duties, both the police officer and the prosecutor have a discretion that must be exercised independently of any outside influence: Krieger v. Law Society of Alberta, [2002] 3 S.C.R. 372, <b>2002 SCC 65</b>. The limits of each official’s discretion are inherent in that person’s role and duties. 
                </p>
