            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                     [1992] 2 S.C.R. 606; Cunningham v. Canada, [1993] 2 S.C.R. 143; R. v. Power, [1994] 1 S.C.R. 601; Krieger v. Law Society of Alberta, [2002] 3 S.C.R. 372, <b>2002 SCC 65</b>; Vriend v. Alberta, [1998] 1 S.C.R. 493; M. v. H., [1999] 2 S.C.R. 3; Guimond v. Quebec (Attorney General), [1996] 3 S.C.R. 347; Winnipeg Child and Family &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;Although this Court has recognized the importance of prosecutorial discretion (see R. v. Power, [1994] 1 S.C.R. 601; Krieger v. Law Society of Alberta, [2002] 3 S.C.R. 372, <b>2002 SCC 65</b>), the constitutional validity of the impugned scheme in this case cannot depend on such discretion. 
